akr 0.1
project legeos

record legeos.work.accessibility-claimed-but-not-built/1 : work {
    title "Either build the AT-SPI bridge and the high-contrast variant, or stop documenting them as shipped"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "distro/seeds/**",
        path "docs/architecture/accessibility.md",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        docs/architecture/accessibility.md states that AT-SPI and Orca ship, and that every shell surface has a high-contrast variant. Neither is true in code. No Cargo.toml in the workspace depends on atspi; no accessible-object tree is registered and no a11y bus is spoken anywhere in shell/lege-shell or crates/. No high-contrast token, theme or setting exists in the shell. distro/seeds/a11y.seed really does install orca, at-spi2-core and speech-dispatcher — which is what makes this worse than a plain omission: a blind user can install LegeOS, start Orca, and meet a desktop that says nothing, because the shell paints itself with a custom rasterizer and exposes no name, role or state for any tile, drawer row or widget region. A toolkit would have carried the bridge; painting everything means owning it.

        What is real: the focus ring is a genuine distinct indicator, reduced motion is a respected setting, and the field is deliberately keyboard-complete with pointer parity enforced by a gate. Those are the foundations; they are not a screen reader.

        Two defensible outcomes and one indefensible one. Build the bridge; or correct the documentation and the seed to say plainly that assistive-technology support is not yet implemented. What must not persist is the current state, where the docs assert a capability a user could depend on and be stranded by. Correcting the documentation costs almost nothing and should not wait for the implementation — that part is separable and worth doing first, whatever is decided about the rest.
        """
    note """
        Ranked fifth for daily-work cost, but the false documentation is a defect independent of that ranking and is cheap to fix on its own.
        """
    derived_from [ @legeos.assessment.home-field-usability-gaps-20260831 ]
    part_of [ @legeos.milestone.m2-session-runs ]
}

record legeos.work.action-manifest-one-declaration-three-surfaces/1 : work {
    title "Declare every user-facing action once, and generate the menu, the cheat sheet and the CLI verb from it"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "cli/lege/**",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/keybindings.rs",
        path "crates/lege-types/**",
        path "shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        LegeOS already commits to every capability being reachable from the CLI, the portal, the shell and Settings (M1 evidence m1-clients-reachable; docs/architecture/accessibility.md: "everything the shell shows is available through lege too"). Today that is a discipline maintained by hand across four implementations, and nothing prevents them drifting. Omarchy demonstrates the mechanism that makes it structural: one declaration — `o.bind(chord, human_label, action)` — is simultaneously the keybinding, the row in a mouse-navigable menu tree, and the entry in a cheat sheet generated from the same table, so the documentation cannot fall out of date with the behaviour because it is not a separate artefact. Adopt the pattern: an action manifest (id, human label, optional chord, CLI verb, availability predicate) living in a service crate rather than the shell, with the shell's menu, the discoverability surface and `lege`'s subcommand table all rendered from it. The manifest belonging to a service rather than the shell is what keeps it consistent with services-not-indicators: the shell renders the manifest, it does not own it.
        """
    note """
        Not M2. What can happen during M2 is that the Home Surface and its information/action rows are built to read rows from a declaration rather than hard-coding them, because retrofitting that later is much more expensive than designing it in — so the cost of deferring the manifest is low only if M2's shell surfaces are written data-driven from the start.

        The keyboard layer is strictly additive, and this is a decision, not an accident of scheduling. Every action must have a mouse or menu path that is not second-class — not a smaller target, not fewer capabilities, not discoverable only by first reading a shortcut list. A chord is an accelerator recorded alongside the menu entry in the same manifest, never the only route to a capability. Omarchy's own navigation manual claims "EVERYTHING happens via the keyboard", and its top-bar manual, documenting full click-and-scroll parity on every widget, contradicts it: read carefully, Omarchy is keyboard-primary with first-class mouse parity, which is the position worth copying rather than the slogan. This is consistent with LegeOS's existing accessibility invariants rather than a change to them.

        See docs/research/omarchy-review.md §3.1 and §5.
        """
    depends_on [ @legeos.constraint.services-not-indicators ]
    source {
        kind internal
        role rationale
        path "docs/research/omarchy-review.md"
        use """
            The study this came from, including the verification pass that corrects three of its own claims against source.
            """
    }
}

record legeos.work.adaptive-files-and-omnibox/1 : work {
    title "WS2 — adaptive Files surface with persistent state, and the federated omnibox"
    state completed
    scope [ path "shell/lege-shell/**" ]
    intent """
        Shell phases 3-4. Phase 3: delete surface/home_screen.rs entirely (the embedded rail and second search field); new pure files_surface.rs with FilesLocation (Directory, Recent, Drives, Trash, Operations, Search; Projects/Network/WindowShelf die as locations) and FilesSurfaceState (location, back/forward stacks, selection, focus, anchor, sort, view, show_hidden, query, scroll) consumed by every rung — navigation and selection semantics ported from apps/lege-files BrowserModel (accepted duplication, papercut it); FileBrowserScreen becomes FilesBrowseView holding no per-screen state, its places sidebar drawn at Full only; draw_expanded becomes breadcrumb band plus files_screen at Representation::Full — one nesting level instead of three; app.rs browse/search state re-keys from Drawer to FilesLocation. Phase 4: new pure omnibox.rs federating apps, projects, files (last completed Files.Search1 result with its query; mismatch renders as still-searching) and declared actions; app_search::MatchKind as the universal strength, source order Project > App > Directory > File > Action, per-source caps before merge, 180ms debounce, stale results dropped, visible rows never reorder on late arrival; scope derived from context (Everything on bare Home, current location inside Files with a permanent Search-everything escape row); delete the launcher widget and its system tile via migrate_launcher_tile (no successor object); launcher.* chords keep working.
        """
    note """
        Verify Tab as the scope-escape key against pixelkit_ui focus traversal before committing; Ctrl-Space is the fallback. Until WS4 lands, the omnibox's project source is Projects1.List; switch to ListDetailed when available.
        """
    acceptance {
        check omnibox-unit-tests {
            statement """
                federate() unit tests pass: cross-source ordering at equal strength, per-source caps before merge, staleness gate, escape row present exactly once under Files scope, empty query lists all offerable apps.
                """
            method command
            command "cargo test -p lege-shell omnibox"
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.ws2-omnibox-unit-tests-20260901/1 ]
        }
        check one-search-control {
            statement """
                Exactly one search field exists in every Home state; typing on bare Home yields federated app, project and file results in deterministic order; late file results never reorder visible rows.
                """
            method manual
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.ws2-one-search-control-20260901/1 ]
        }
        check state-continuity {
            statement """
                Expanding Files, navigating three directories deep, selecting entries, collapsing and re-expanding lands in the same directory with the same selection and scroll.
                """
            method manual
            verified_by [ @legeos.evidence.ws2-state-continuity-20260901/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @legeos.work.home-presentation-subtraction ]
    implements [ @legeos.decision.representation-ladder-is-the-organizing-principle ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.design-consolidation-roadmap ]
}

record legeos.work.adaptive-index-maintenance/1 : work {
    title "Adaptive persistent index maintenance — coverage tiers, split freshness, a watcher that sleeps, and a measured backend choice"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "cli/lege/**",
        path "crates/lege-file-index/**",
        path "docs/measurements/**",
        path "interfaces/dbus/**",
        path "scripts/**",
        path "services/lege-filed/**",
        path "services/lege-hostd/**",
        path "tools/xtask/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Keep the shared file index persistent while making its maintenance adaptive: direct write-through when lege-filed performs the operation, kernel events while a consumer is observing, and filesystem metadata deltas to bridge dormant periods, with no recurring crawler in normal operation. Separate acquisition coverage (what the OS knows exists) from client visibility (what a search returns), so a drive visualiser can size target/ while filename search never returns build artefacts. Split namespace freshness from metadata freshness so a filename query stops paying for a per-child stat. Remove the 500ms idle wake and wire the Index1 change signals, which never fire in the current build. Choose the live event backend (inotify, unprivileged fanotify, privileged filesystem-wide fanotify) and the dormant-period bridge (Btrfs send delta vs recursive reconciliation) on measurements taken on the reference machine, not on assumption.
        """
    note """
        Plan of record for this campaign: ~/.claude/plans/implement-a-plan-based-fizzy-journal.md, which extends docs/planning/shared-incremental-file-index.md rather than replacing it. Six lanes in three waves over one shared tree. Measured premise: $HOME holds 230,323 directories (189,089 after the current skip list) against a WatchBudget of 125,037, so recursive inotify cannot reach the intended steady state on the machine the design was written for. The development host is ext4 end-to-end and LegeOS is not yet installed, so all Btrfs work is validated against loopback images with @/@home/@snapshots subvolumes; agents have no sudo, so privileged steps are collected into scripts the user runs.
        """
    depends_on [ @legeos.work.file-index-core, @legeos.work.file-index-maintenance ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.shared-incremental-file-index ]
}

record legeos.work.application-integration-interim/1 : work {
    title "Interim application integration — the Terminal role and the Super+grave drop-down terminal, the Files context menu, the search panel with FSearch as the FileSearch provider, Kitty defaults"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "apps/lege-files/**",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/keybindings.rs",
        path "crates/lege-appd/**",
        path "crates/lege-session-core/**",
        path "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs",
        path "crates/lege-types/src/provider.rs",
        path "debian/**",
        path "distro/**",
        path "services/lege-sessiond/**",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Integration choices the user made on 2026-09-03, revised the same day. (a) Terminal: ProviderRole::Terminal, exclusive, candidates from TerminalEmulator desktop entries, default kitty; the shell relays the selection with lege_shell_manager_v1.set_terminal and answers terminal_requested by launching the provider through the launch chain; Super+grave (compositor chord; a bare ~ or ` is a typed character and cannot be a global chord) toggles the terminal: launch when none, unshelve/activate when shelved or unfocused, shelve and refocus the previous window when focused, never close. No Ctrl+Alt+T. 'Open terminal here' in the Files context menu launches it with --directory. /etc/xdg/kitty/kitty.conf shipped by legeos-shell with the LegeOS palette, background_opacity 0.85, remember_window_size no, wayland_titlebar_color system, overridable by ~/.config/kitty. (b) Files object: secondary-click menu on rows and empty space reusing surface/context_menu.rs and the declared files.* verbs (Open, Open with…, Open terminal here, Copy path, Rename, Move to trash, Properties; New folder, Paste, Select all, Sort by…, Show hidden), then multi-select, keyboard verbs, drag-and-drop and properties-as-pane. (c) Search: the top field on Home stays the application launcher only (COSMIC's launcher shape); real search is the Files object's search field expanded into a partial-screen panel (about two thirds of the field, centred, hairline edge) over lege-filed's scoped index with path, size and modified time per row and keyboard navigation, whose last row is 'Search everywhere with FSearch'; ProviderRole::FileSearch, exclusive, default FSearch, launched with --search <query>; 'Search here…' in the context menu; lege-filed keeps its index; later sessiond feeds fsearch's include list from Files.Search1.Roots.
        """
    note """
        Plan text: docs/planning/window-groups-and-application-integration.md §4.2–4.6 (ea9810e). Implementation started 2026-09-03 in one lane: Terminal role → set_terminal/terminal_requested relay → Files context menu → search panel + FileSearch role → kitty.conf → multi-select if time. Terminal=true desktop-entry wrapping and Super+F are deferred. Not planned: reading fsearch's database or replacing Files.Search1.
        """
    depends_on [ @legeos.work.wayland-clipboard-and-dnd-transport ]
    implements [ @legeos.decision.the-home-field-is-a-launcher-not-a-search ]
    part_of [ @legeos.work.home-surface-usability-next ]
}

record legeos.work.application-integration-interim/2 : work {
    title "Interim application integration — the Terminal role and the Super+grave drop-down terminal, the Files context menu, the search panel with FSearch as the FileSearch provider, Kitty defaults"
    state active
    scope [
        path "apps/lege-files/**",
        path "compositor/lege-comp/src/keybindings.rs",
        path "crates/lege-appd/**",
        path "crates/lege-session-core/**",
        path "crates/lege-types/src/action.rs",
